;; Agents (v1: seon.config.agent.edn's max-running and inbox-size, ported; max-turns was
;; next's task config key; the rest are new in next, where an agent is a forked process).
{
 ;; Unit: runs running at once. Provenance: owner, 2026-10-29 ("10-20 should be fine for users
 ;; with small machines"): an agent costs ~20.6 MB of real memory (bin/check scale, 2026-10-29), so
 ;; ten fit an 8 GB machine beside the user's work (was 4, v1's, when an agent was counted by its
 ;; footprint); the machine's RAM sizes it. A ready task beyond it
 ;; waits for an end; 0 starts nothing new while running work finishes. root's start! may
 ;; pass :max-running for one run; the fact on main says what the run used.
 :seon.config.agent/max-running [:int {:default 10 :min 0 :seon.config/unit "runs" :description "Runs running at once; a ready task beyond it waits for an end. 0 starts nothing new."}]
 ;; Unit: addresses. Where every agent is told to look for more, beside its task's citations (the owner, 2026-11-05: "include some
 ;; references they can use to get more information about completing tasks or merging or whatever else is secondary and we aren't
 ;; showing right away"). Each is one line in the agent's "If you need more" part (seon.render.ui/references): the section's heading, a
 ;; sentence of its lead and the form that reads it; none is shown whole. Written as the agent's own cite entities by its first run on
 ;; its branch (seon.task/agent-cites-tx, seon.agent/begin-run!); the agent changes its own with my.agent/cite! and uncite!. An empty
 ;; vector gives it none.
 :seon.config.agent/cites [:vector {:default ["docs/agent.md#working-a-task" "docs/agent.md#merging" "docs/testing.md#testing-in-the-world" "docs/data-modeling.md#data-modeling"] :seon.config/unit "addresses" :description "What every agent's context reads beside its task's citations, written as its own cites by its first run on its branch (seon.task/agent-cites-tx, seon.agent/begin-run!): addresses, each a path with an optional ?depth (summary, lead or full) and #anchor (a page whole with none), or a name."} :string]
 ;; Unit: levels (seon.config.agent.edn). (describe @ctx) at 2 shows the record and its task one level in, about 25 entries.
 :seon.config.agent/describe-depth [:int {:default 2 :min 1 :seon.config/unit "levels" :description "How many levels deep (my.agent/describe v) describes a value when it is asked no depth: each level its own entries, a deeper one by (describe v path)."}]
 ;; Unit: turns a run may take when its task sets none (a task's own
 ;; :seon.config.agent/max-turns wins). At the bound the run ends and the done rule judges it.
 ;; Provenance: v1's live runs measured turns-to-green median 14-40, run 11's p90 32
 ;; (2026-10-18); R4's DeepSeek runs p90 6 with good context. 8 cut ordinary runs below
 ;; v1's median (owner, 2026-10-21: "8 turn cap? wtf? make sensible defaults!"), so the bound
 ;; sits above the measured p90. Tokens are the paid resource; task
 ;; seon.model/budgets-are-data-and-spend-is-measured makes them the budget.
 :seon.config.agent/max-turns [:int {:default 40 :min 1 :seon.config/unit "turns" :description "Turns a run may take when its task sets none."}]
 ;; Unit: turns. What a wake adds to an agent's own budget (:seon.agent/max-turns), in the write that starts it, whoever sent the message
 ;; that woke it (owner, 2026-11-07: "lets configure a wake allowance that increases the max turns and that is the main mechanism that
 ;; controls the agentic loop"). At the bound the agent ends :cap as before and waits for the next wake. Provenance: a first value,
 ;; a quarter of max-turns, from no measurement yet; the turns a woken agent spends answering are recorded per wake
 ;; (:seon.agent/turn against its budget), and this moves with them. Two agents that answer each other spend this per wake each.
 :seon.config.agent/wake-turns [:int {:default 10 :min 1 :seon.config/unit "turns" :description "Turns a wake adds to the agent's own budget (:seon.agent/max-turns), written with the start the wake makes: a message that reaches an agent waiting (parked, told or at its cap) raises the budget by this, whoever sent it. The main control of the agentic loop: an agent that is not messaged is never woken."}]
 ;; Unit: path, every agent process's working directory, shared, made at the fork server's boot.
 ;; No repository above it: an agent changes code through its REPL, never git (owner, 2026-10-22:
 ;; "no git in agents"); it reads and writes any file by its absolute path. At the bound: n/a.
 :seon.config.agent/cwd [:string {:default "/tmp/seon-agents" :seon.config/place true :min 1 :seon.config/unit "path" :description "Every agent process's working directory, with no repository above it."}]
 ;; Unit: messages in each agent channel's buffer (v1's :seon.config.agent/inbox-size 64).
 ;; Small on purpose: a full channel pushes back on its sender (the reader leaves frames in
 ;; the socket).
 :seon.config.agent/inbox-size [:int {:default 64 :min 1 :seon.config/unit "messages" :description "Each agent channel's buffer; a full channel pushes back on its sender."}]
 ;; Unit: bytes allocated between an agent's collections (Chez's collect-trip-bytes). Chez's
 ;; default scales with the parent heap (E10); a small trip keeps an agent's garbage, and so
 ;; its private pages, small.
 :seon.config.agent/trip-bytes [:int {:default 4194304 :min 65536 :seon.config/unit "bytes" :description "Chez's collect-trip-bytes in an agent: allocation between collections."}]
 ;; Unit: bytes an agent's heap may grow past what it inherited. Past it an allocation throws
 ;; OutOfMemoryError in the form (an :err item), so a runaway agent cannot take the machine.
 :seon.config.agent/heap-extra-bytes [:int {:default 1073741824 :min 1048576 :seon.config/unit "bytes" :description "How far an agent's heap may grow past what it inherited; past it an allocation throws OutOfMemoryError."}]
 ;; Unit: bytes of each unix socket's send and receive buffer (root, the fork server and every
 ;; agent). Provenance: T1, 1 MB makes a 1 MB reply cost what the ring's did. At the bound a
 ;; writer waits for the reader (frames are never dropped).
 :seon.config.agent/sock-buf-bytes [:int {:default 1048576 :seon.config/at-load true :min 4096 :seon.config/unit "bytes" :description "Each socket's send and receive buffer (root, fork server and agents)."}]
 ;; Unit: bytes. What one form the model, an outside agent or the page sends may allocate before it
 ;; stops with :seon.repl/bytes-spent (jolt.host/run-interruptible's byte budget, checked at its tick
 ;; polls, so a stop can land one poll's allocation past it). Provenance: md-scan #9's 17 model evals
 ;; (the optimization session, 2026-10-29): median 1.5 MB, p90 ~66 MB, max 90 MB; 1 GiB is ~11x the
 ;; largest. The agent's own loop (seon.turn/work!) has none. 0: none. Set to none by the owner's
 ;; ruling, 2026-10-30 ("Please remove the 1GB budget. That's way too low"): md-scan #28's median eval
 ;; allocated 47 MB, a register! or edit! 160-230 MB, and the fixed 1 GiB stopped root's whole-program
 ;; loads, a prove and a heap test; an eval's budget is to come from measured evals (task
 ;; seon.agent/an-evals-budget-comes-from-measured-evals).
 :seon.config.agent/form-bytes [:int {:default 0 :min 0 :seon.config/unit "bytes" :description "What one form the model, an outside agent or the page sends may allocate before it stops with :seon.repl/bytes-spent. 0: none."}]
 ;; Unit: engine ticks (procedure calls and loop back-edges: work done, never time) one form
 ;; an agent's model or an outside agent sends may run (Jolt's run-interruptible tick budget).
 ;; Provenance: session AGENTS, measured on Jolt 2026-10-21: a tight loop ~4e9 ticks/s, an
 ;; allocating one ~7.5e8, so this stops a runaway form after ~5-25 s of work. At the bound
 ;; the form answers the typed :seon.repl/ticks-spent and the request goes on. 0: none.
 :seon.config.agent/form-ticks [:int {:default 20000000000 :min 0 :seon.config/unit "engine ticks" :description "Work one form the model or an outside agent sends may do before it stops with :seon.repl/ticks-spent. 0: none."}]
 ;; Unit: lines. How many lines before and after the changed text seon.repl/edit! answers, so the
 ;; agent sees its edit land without reading the definition again. Provenance: the md-scan bench,
 ;; 2026-10-29 (run 5 reread a 250-line scan after each change); two, as a diff's context reads. At
 ;; the bound the rest of the definition is left out of the answer, its (source f) whole.
 :seon.config.agent/edit-context-lines [:int {:default 2 :min 0 :seon.config/unit "lines" :description "How many lines before and after the changed text seon.repl/edit! answers."}]
 ;; Unit: functions. How many of a form's costliest contracted functions (seon.profile/top)
 ;; its reply item and evaluation record keep, each with its exact calls and estimated ms.
 ;; Provenance: worktree session F2's literal 10, made a key (research row top-fns, 2026-10-22). At the
 ;; bound the rest are left out of the item; the record says nothing of them.
 :seon.config.agent/form-top-fns [:int {:default 10 :min 0 :seon.config/unit "functions" :description "How many of a form's costliest contracted functions its reply item keeps (seon.repl/wrap-cost)."}]
 ;; Unit: engine ticks ONE test (its :each fixtures included) may run, wherever it runs: an
 ;; agent's turn, the judge, bin/test (seon.proofs/run-tests!). One bound, per test, so a test's
 ;; verdict is a function of the test alone and the same in every place, and a green reused by
 ;; its digest means the same thing everywhere (task seon.proofs/one-prove-path; it replaced a
 ;; per-run bound and a turn's own smaller per-run bound, under which
 ;; a proof could be red in its turn and green at the judge). Larger than a form's: cost proofs
 ;; measure large sizes. Provenance: the per-run judge bound it was (no single in-process test
 ;; needed more); each result records its :ticks. At the bound that test is an :error of
 ;; :seon.test/ticks-spent naming it, and the run goes on. 0: none. Lowered 2026-10-29 (owner: "I don't
 ;; want long running tests period and I want to catch the problems sooner"; and "What should the longest test ever be? make
 ;; that config a lot smaller"): the newest greens of 2,139 tests read p50 1.0 M, p99 164 M, p99.5 219 M ticks, the costliest real
 ;; one 252 M (seon.ctx-test's 5.4 G implied-joins test fixed at its cause, 36 M), so 300 M ends a loop in about 3 s and fails
 ;; none; it caps a stated estimate's budget too (ten times the estimate, never past this).
 ;; While :seon.config.proofs/ticks-decide is off (a test's count is not its own work yet), the ceiling is 10G again: a runaway loop
 ;; still ends (about a minute), and no honest test reaches it (the costliest real one 252M). 300M (from percentiles, not
 ;; arithmetic) redded 8 honest tests at 7cf0c01b64; restated by arithmetic when seon.proofs/a-tests-ticks-are-its-own-work lands.
 :seon.config.agent/test-ticks [:int {:default 10000000000 :min 0 :seon.config/unit "engine ticks" :description "Work one test may do, wherever it runs (a turn, the judge, bin/test); past it that test is an :error of :seon.test/ticks-spent naming it, and the run goes on. 0: none."}]
 ;; Unit: ms. How long a fork waits for every other thread to park (seon.lib/fork!, the test child's
 ;; remote.flow/fork!; Chez fork_alone) before it answers refused, naming the threads that held it. A thread in a C
 ;; call that is not collect-safe never parks, and with no bound such a fork waited for good (a test child,
 ;; 2026-11-14). Provenance: 100 forks right after a wake waited 3.9-4.6 ms median, 6.4 ms at most, fork(2)
 ;; included (load 15, a small process, 2026-11-14); root's fork(2) alone is 65 ms (docs/cost.md); about ten times that.
 :seon.config.agent/fork-wait-ms [:int {:default 1000 :min 1 :seon.config/unit "ms" :description "How long a fork waits for every other thread to park before it answers refused, naming the threads that held it (seon.lib/fork!, Chez fork_alone)."}]
 ;; The silence, ms, after which an agent with a request in flight is observed stalled, once per
 ;; quiet spell (seon.agent-proc's stall check), and a test bin/test runs is named STALLED: a
 ;; recorded fact (:seon.agent/stall, an open :stalled problem), never an end. Provenance: the
 ;; guessed tolerance the agent proc held as a var (30 s; a model call answers or streams within
 ;; it). Past it nothing is ended. 0: no check.
 :seon.config.agent/stall-ms [:int {:default 30000 :seon.config/at-load true :min 0 :seon.config/unit "ms" :description "Silence after which an agent with a request in flight is observed stalled, and a running bin/test test is named STALLED; recorded, never an end. 0: no check."}]}
